@using Memoria.Web.Extensibility

@* The line under a retired type's name saying so, in the words the domain retired it with. One
   component rather than a copy under each heading: the same sentence is true of a retired
   aggregate, projection, event, stream and identifier, and a reader who meets it on one page
   should meet the same mark and the same shape on the next.

   Drawn only when the type is retired and nothing otherwise, so every heading can ask for it and
   a heading over a live type is left exactly as it was. The mark in front is the one the list
   rows carry after the name, so what was clicked and what arrived look like the same thing.

   A caution in the page's own sense of the word: nothing failed and nothing is wrong with the
   type. It is bound, its rows still read, and the note is the domain saying not to write any
   more of them. *@
@if (DomainTypeDescriber.ObsoleteOf(Type) is { } obsolete)
{
    <p class="obsolete">
        <svg class="glyph" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
             fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
             stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7.1"/>
            <path d="M5 5l10 10"/>
        </svg>
        @obsolete
    </p>
}

@code {

    /// <summary>The type the note is under.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public Type Type { get; set; } = null!;

}
